  "partyLabels": "none-recorded",
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919: the first county council election in the five electoral areas that replaced the county electoral divisions of 1899, 21 seats. Polled Thursday 3 June 1920 (Belfast News-Letter, 3 June 1920, p. 6, \"To-day's Polling\"); counted at the Courthouse, Crumlin Road, Belfast, by Alexander Millar, secretary of the county council, as returning officer. Antrim area (four seats): 6,579 valid votes, quota 1,316; 12,689 electors. Count 2, Minford's surplus of 850: Lawther 501, Dickie 313, Laverty 19, O'Neill 17. Count 3, M'Manus's surplus of 50: Dickie 46, Laverty 3, O'Neill 1. Count 4, Lawther's surplus of 198: Dickie 185, Laverty 11, O'Neill 2. Count 5, O'Neill's 452 votes: Laverty 346, Dickie 13 (the paper prints no non-transferable figure; 93 is the remainder). Dickie was elected on the fifth count with 1,287, below the quota. Elected in the order Minford, M'Manus, Lawther, Dickie. The minute of the council's first meeting also records the chairmen of the rural district councils, who sat ex officio, and the co-opted members; they were not elected and are not listed here."
}
